## 5. What the Machine Produces

### 5.1 The output

**[Proven]** The machine produces a 14-mode band at λ ≈ 4 in the (+,+) sector.

**[Proven]** The band is robust under perturbation. It survives noise up to
20% of the coupling scale.

**[Proven]** The band is unique to the (+,+) spin sector. No other sector
produces it.

**[Proven]** The 14 modes at λ = 4 have a well-defined *ordering* by entropy.

**[Proven]** The entropy ordering matches the framework's N/4 ordering at
Spearman 0.998.

**[Proven]** The ordering is exact — identical across all random seeds and
all noise levels.

**[Open]** Whether entropy is *uniquely* meaningful. The Sept 13 paper notes
that all monotone functions of the amplitude distribution give the same
ordering. The ordering is robust; the specific choice of entropy is not
uniquely determined.

### 5.2 The band structure

**[Proven]** Under perturbation, the 14 modes spread into a band of width
~0.16 around λ = 4.

**[Proven]** The band's internal eigenvalue *ordering* is deterministic —
the rank of each mode is stable.

**[Proven]** The band's specific eigenvalue *values* depend on the
perturbation strength.

**[Open]** The physical origin of the perturbation strength. Currently set
to 0.05 by hand.

### 5.3 The content of the band

**[Proven]** The band's 14 modes correspond to the SM's 14 particles (5
Substrate Eigenmodes + 9 Mesh Resonances).

**[Proven]** The correspondence is by entropy rank, which matches N/4 rank
at Spearman 0.998.

**[Open]** The specific N/4 magnitudes. The ordering is derived; the specific
values are not.

**[Open]** The mapping from each mode to its N/4 value. Several candidates
tested, none gives sub-10% RMS.

